<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1041</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Kakuya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ala al-Dawla Muhammad, known as Ibn Kakuya, was the founder of the Kakuyid dynasty. Appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> around 1008, he made himself independent ruler of Isfahan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> served at his court as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> and physician and wrote major works there. He was repeatedly pressed by the Ghaznavids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ala-al-dawla-muhammad.jpg" alt="Ala al-Dawla Muhammad" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MuhammadibnRustamDushmanziyarCoin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad was the son of Rustam Dushmanziyar, a Daylamite soldier in Buyid service who was given land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alborz">Alborz</a> and charged with protecting Ray and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jibal">Jibal</a> against the local leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>. Rustam's sister was the Bavandid princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyida-shirin">Sayyida Shirin</a>, mother of the Buyid emirs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shams-al-dawla">Shams al-Dawla</a>, and Muhammad's names Ibn Kakuya and Pusar-i Kaku come from kaku, meaning maternal uncle in the Daylami language. These family ties explain his post as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> by the Daylamite officer Ibn Fuladh in 1016, he soon regained it, and the fragility of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a>'s kingdom encouraged him to expand. In 1023 he seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> from the Buyid ruler Sama' al-Dawla and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinavar">Dinavar</a> and Shapur-Khwast from Kurdish leaders. Five years later he won a victory at Nihawand, capturing the Bavandid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ja">Abu Ja</a>'far Muhammad and his two sons. Though Majd al-Dawla remained his overlord, he minted coins in his own name and received from the Abbasid caliph al-Qadir the title Sword of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>.</p><p>After the Ghaznavid Mahmud deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majd-al-dawla">Majd al-Dawla</a> in 1029, Mahmud's son Mas'ud I defeated Ala al-Dawla, who fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a> but soon returned as a Ghaznavid vassal paying an annual tribute of 200,000 dinars. He briefly occupied Ray in 1030, was defeated by Mas'ud again in 1035 and began recruiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> to regain his lands. In 1037/38 he again took Ray, and the walls he then built around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> saved it from the Turkmen nomads who plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> and other places in 1038/39.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ala al-Dawla died in September 1041 while campaigning in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> against the Annazids. His eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faramurz">Faramurz</a> succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and his younger son Garshasp I gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, but they had a difficult task defending these lands against the expanding Seljuqs. At his court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a>, who came after leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>, wrote an encyclopedia dedicated to him. After Avicenna's death in 1037 his library was carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> by the Ghaznavids and later destroyed there by the Ghurids under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-al-din-husayn">Ala al-Din Husayn</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ala al-Dawla Muhammad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ala al-Dawla Muhammad</b>, called Ibn Kakuya because he was the maternal uncle (kaku) of the Buyid regent Sayyida, was appointed governor of Isfahan around 1008.</blockquote><a href="/state/kakuyid-dynasty">Kakuyid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
